<h2>Fees and the final cost</h2>
<p>Seat Outlet is a resale marketplace. Prices are set by sellers and may be above or below face value. Fees and taxes, such as service or delivery fees, can be added at checkout, so the price on a listing is not always the final cost. Review the full cost at checkout before you pay, and compare totals, not just the first number you see. For background on what regulators expect, the FTC explains its rule on <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/rule-unfair-or-deceptive-fees-frequently-asked-questions" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unfair or deceptive fees</a>.</p>

<h2>Resale marketplaces</h2>
<p>Ticket holders who cannot attend may resell, and on a marketplace the seller sets the price. That means a resale ticket can cost more than face value for a hot show and less when demand is soft. Seat Outlet does not set seller prices and does not promise the lowest price. For background, see this overview of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ticket_resale" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ticket resale</a>. Policies on resale money for artists or organizers vary by event and region.</p>
<p>Event schedules and release timelines are decided by organizers, performers and promoters. Automated buying is a known problem in ticketing, and our <a href="/terms-and-conditions">terms and conditions</a> prohibit bots and scrapers for bulk ticket purchasing.</p>

<h2>How to pay less</h2>
<ol>
<li>Compare several sections and rows for the same event.</li>
<li>Check the total with fees, not only the listed price.</li>
<li>Look at <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a> and try a <a href="/tickets-promo-code">promo code</a> if one is available.</li>
<li>Be flexible on date, or browse <a href="/concert-tickets-for-sale">concert tickets</a> for a different show.</li>
<li>Buy where you have protection: see <a href="/worry-free-guarantee">our guarantee</a> and <a href="/ticket-buyer-protection">buyer protection</a>.</li>
</ol>
<p>Questions about delivery or entry? The <a href="/ticket-faq">ticket FAQ</a> has short answers. Want a plain-English walk through a purchase? Read <a href="/how-to-buy-tickets-online">how to buy tickets online</a>.</p>

<h2>Questions</h2>
<details class="so-faq"><summary>Why are prices high for some shows and low for others?</summary><p>Prices depend on demand, seat location, event popularity and availability, plus fees. Popular tours with few dates tend to cost the most.</p></details>
<details class="so-faq"><summary>Are resale tickets always more expensive than face value?</summary><p>No. Sellers set resale prices, so they can be above or below face value.</p></details>
<details class="so-faq"><summary>Where can I find a better price?</summary><p>Compare sections, check totals, and watch <a href="/ticket-deals">ticket deals</a>. No marketplace can promise the lowest price for every event.</p></details>
